1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:12,530
The first ship took seven months to build and
it was not built by the System.

2
00:00:12,929 --> 00:00:19,018
The narrator wishes to be exact, because it is the
point of this chapter and the reason

3
00:00:19,038 --> 00:00:22,880
Albert Wesker gave an engineer from
the Arcology a pen.

4
00:00:23,280 --> 00:00:29,579
The System sold him the hull pattern and the drive
geometry: nine billion Points and forty

5
00:00:29,599 --> 00:00:35,547
billion Points, delivered as specification —
thousands of pages of it, in a form that

6
00:00:35,567 --> 00:00:41,586
Crane's people described as *written by somebody who
has never had to explain anything

7
00:00:41,606 --> 00:00:42,850
to anybody twice*.

8
00:00:43,250 --> 00:00:48,848
It did not sell him a ship. He could have bought one.
There was a line in the BEYOND tab

9
00:00:48,868 --> 00:00:54,274
that would have put a finished four-kilometre
vessel in orbit overnight, crewed, with

10
00:00:54,294 --> 00:00:57,083
a maintenance log going back
nineteen years.

11
00:00:57,484 --> 00:01:03,317
He did not buy it. And when Mira Halder asked him
why, in the second week, on a gantry,

12
00:01:03,337 --> 00:01:08,834
in a pressure suit, he gave her the only fully
honest answer of the whole account.

13
00:01:09,353 --> 00:01:16,490
Because everything I have ever been given arrived
finished, and because I have spent sixty-four

14
00:01:16,510 --> 00:01:19,504
years watching what that does to people.

15
00:01:19,784 --> 00:01:26,785
There are four hundred thousand human beings on this
planet who cannot refuse an instruction,

16
00:01:26,805 --> 00:01:33,278
and there are four hundred thousand more in an ocean
who have never had a consequence,

17
00:01:33,298 --> 00:01:39,394
and the common cause of both is that I was in a
hurry and the catalogue was open.

18
00:01:39,674 --> 00:01:47,304
(very cold) So this one gets built. By people.
Slowly. With arguments about the budget.

19
00:01:47,703 --> 00:01:51,293
It's going to take seven months and it's
going to have a flaw in it.

20
00:01:51,694 --> 00:01:58,093
Yes. And in four hundred years somebody will find
the flaw and know that a person put

21
00:01:58,113 --> 00:02:04,664
it there, and that will be worth more to them than a
ship that came out of a catalogue.

22
00:02:05,063 --> 00:02:11,246
They called it the *Christina*, after a river in
Delaware that four hundred thousand people

23
00:02:11,266 --> 00:02:14,313
in the middle of the Atlantic
had never seen.

24
00:02:14,713 --> 00:02:20,864
Four kilometres. Crew of eleven hundred. A drive that
changed velocity without expelling

25
00:02:20,884 --> 00:02:27,034
mass, built to a geometry nobody on earth understood
and four people could operate. Nine

26
00:02:27,054 --> 00:02:32,993
hundred and forty habitation decks, a hospital,
four hydroponic halls — and a garden,

27
00:02:33,013 --> 00:02:39,304
on deck twenty-six, laid out by a woman on an ice
shelf who had sent one line in a letter.

28
00:02:39,704 --> 00:02:45,445
Mira Halder was chief of structures. She argued
with the Directorate about the budget

29
00:02:45,465 --> 00:02:51,342
nineteen times and won twenty-six, which she
described afterwards as *the best nineteen

30
00:02:51,362 --> 00:02:52,834
arguments of my life*.

31
00:02:53,234 --> 00:02:58,764
The narrator will give you one of them, because it is
the moment the story changes hands.

32
00:02:59,283 --> 00:03:02,353
I want the redundancy on the
drive spars doubled.

33
00:03:02,753 --> 00:03:06,403
The System's specification does
not require it.

34
00:03:06,804 --> 00:03:12,235
The System's specification assumes a hull that was
placed rather than welded. Mine has

35
00:03:12,255 --> 00:03:17,939
four thousand human welds in it and I know exactly how
good my welders are, which is very,

36
00:03:17,959 --> 00:03:18,953
and not perfect.

37
00:03:19,354 --> 00:03:22,783
It is eighteen billion Points
and four months.

38
00:03:23,183 --> 00:03:25,993
Then it's eighteen billion Points
and four months.

39
00:03:26,834 --> 00:03:34,134
(very cold) Ms. Halder, are you aware that you are
the only person alive who argues with

40
00:03:34,154 --> 00:03:34,383
me?

41
00:03:34,783 --> 00:03:39,013
Yes. Everybody's aware. There's a rota — I'm
joking. There isn't a rota.

42
00:03:39,623 --> 00:03:44,380
Director, I've thought about this a lot, because
people keep telling me I'm brave and

43
00:03:44,400 --> 00:03:49,324
I'm not. I argue with you because you have never
once, in two years, made me pay for it.

44
00:03:49,344 --> 00:03:54,100
Mr. Carrick told me you've been like this since
2033 and that it's the only reason he

45
00:03:54,120 --> 00:03:54,493
stayed.

46
00:03:54,774 --> 00:03:59,555
(very slow) I think you've spent sixty-five years
building an empire that cannot say no

47
00:03:59,575 --> 00:04:04,412
to you and hiring, one at a time, the four or five
people who can. And I don't think you

48
00:04:04,432 --> 00:04:06,123
ever noticed you were doing it.

49
00:04:06,524 --> 00:04:13,040
The narrator notes that Albert Wesker approved the
doubled redundancy without further

50
00:04:13,060 --> 00:04:19,884
discussion, that it cost eighteen billion Points and
four months, and that in 2134 a spar

51
00:04:19,904 --> 00:04:26,421
failure on a sister ship killed nine hundred people
and the *Christina* class was the

52
00:04:26,441 --> 00:04:30,574
only design in the fleet that
survived the same fault.

53
00:04:30,973 --> 00:04:36,478
The narrator would like to record one argument from
the seven months, because it is the

54
00:04:36,498 --> 00:04:40,923
argument that decided what kind of thing was
leaving the solar system.

55
00:04:41,324 --> 00:04:47,599
The *Christina*'s crew was eleven hundred. The
Directorate's original manifest, drafted

56
00:04:47,619 --> 00:04:54,183
by Hale's successor and approved without discussion,
was eleven hundred Absolute personnel:

57
00:04:54,203 --> 00:05:00,334
incapable of panic, incapable of mutiny, incapable
of deciding at year nine that they

58
00:05:00,354 --> 00:05:01,854
would rather go home.

59
00:05:02,254 --> 00:05:08,469
Mira Halder read it and went to the Assembly instead
of to Wesker, which is the single

60
00:05:08,489 --> 00:05:11,224
most consequential thing she ever did.

61
00:05:11,744 --> 00:05:17,045
You are proposing to put the first human beings
outside this system in the hands of eleven

62
00:05:17,065 --> 00:05:22,367
hundred people who cannot refuse an order, four
hundred light years from anybody who could

63
00:05:22,387 --> 00:05:24,673
ask them whether they want to be there.

64
00:05:24,954 --> 00:05:29,664
The Accord says we are releasing them. It does
not say we may spend them first.

65
00:05:30,064 --> 00:05:36,856
The Assembly voted four hundred and one to ten. The
crew of the *Christina* was recruited:

66
00:05:36,876 --> 00:05:43,668
nineteen thousand applications for eleven hundred
places, assessed by a board Mira chaired

67
00:05:43,688 --> 00:05:45,334
and Wesker was not on.

68
00:05:45,733 --> 00:05:51,500
Four of the eleven hundred were released Absolute
personnel who applied like everybody

69
00:05:51,520 --> 00:05:57,220
else. One of them, a former maintenance technician
from the Newark plant who had been

70
00:05:57,240 --> 00:06:03,276
made on a Saturday in 2027 and released in the second
cohort in 2082, was the ship's chief

71
00:06:03,296 --> 00:06:09,063
of environmental systems for forty-one years and is
buried on Ochoa under a stone that

72
00:06:09,083 --> 00:06:12,024
says, at his own instruction,
*he chose it*.

73
00:06:12,423 --> 00:06:19,097
Owen Carrick died on the nineteenth of October 2091,
aged ninety-three, at home, having

74
00:06:19,117 --> 00:06:25,945
declined Aeterna for the last twenty-two years of his
life for reasons he never explained

75
00:06:25,965 --> 00:06:32,331
to anyone except his wife, who had taken it and
who outlived him by eleven decades.

76
00:06:32,730 --> 00:06:39,313
Wesker came on the Thursday. It was the ninth folder,
and it was four sentences, and Carrick

77
00:06:39,333 --> 00:06:42,040
made him read it out loud in the room.

78
00:06:42,560 --> 00:06:43,850
(very slow) Read it.

79
00:06:44,250 --> 00:06:50,851
*Fifty-eight years. He never once removed a person for
telling him the truth, and he never

80
00:06:50,871 --> 00:06:53,940
once changed course because of it either.*

81
00:06:54,221 --> 00:07:00,710
*I have concluded that these are the same fact and
not two, and that a man who cannot

82
00:07:00,730 --> 00:07:03,391
be angered also cannot be reached.*

83
00:07:03,670 --> 00:07:10,245
*I was the mechanism that should have stopped this
and I did not work, and I am not sure

84
00:07:10,265 --> 00:07:16,540
any mechanism would have, and I am too tired now to
know whether that is an excuse.*

85
00:07:16,821 --> 00:07:20,111
*Tell Mira. She is the one who might.*

86
00:07:21,170 --> 00:07:23,021
(very slow) You didn't argue.

87
00:07:23,420 --> 00:07:24,951
(very soft) No.

88
00:07:25,350 --> 00:07:28,701
(very slow) Fifty-eight years and
that's the first time.

89
00:07:29,100 --> 00:07:36,204
Mr. Carrick, in 2033 you sat in a diner and told me
you were taking the job so that you

90
00:07:36,224 --> 00:07:43,491
could be the one who told me no. You have done it
nine times. I have overruled you eight.

91
00:07:43,891 --> 00:07:45,081
(very slow) Eight?

92
00:07:45,480 --> 00:07:52,026
The ninth was in January 2040 and I wrote *you are
correct and I have no answer* at the

93
00:07:52,046 --> 00:07:58,441
bottom of it, and I have never told you, and I am
telling you now because there is no

94
00:07:58,461 --> 00:08:04,931
longer any tactical reason not to and I have
discovered that I would like you to know.

95
00:08:05,880 --> 00:08:11,679
The narrator records that Owen Carrick laughed,
which hurt him, and that he said four

96
00:08:11,699 --> 00:08:17,567
more words, and that Albert Wesker sat in that room
for another two hours after he had

97
00:08:17,587 --> 00:08:18,731
stopped speaking.

98
00:08:19,250 --> 00:08:21,500
(very slow) Fifty-eight years. One.

99
00:08:21,901 --> 00:08:28,130
He is buried in Media, Pennsylvania, in an ordinary
cemetery, under an ordinary stone.

100
00:08:28,150 --> 00:08:34,596
The Umbrella Directorate flew its flag at half mast
for the second time in its existence.

101
00:08:34,998 --> 00:08:42,184
His nine folders are in a drawer in a desk in a
building on the Christina River that appeared

102
00:08:42,204 --> 00:08:49,158
out of nothing on the fourteenth of September 2026.
They were published, in full, in 2094,

103
00:08:49,178 --> 00:08:52,568
on Wesker's instruction,
without commentary.

104
00:08:52,967 --> 00:08:56,197
They are the primary source for about a
third of this account.

105
00:08:56,597 --> 00:09:01,936
The narrator will give you the departure, because it
is the last scene in this Part and

106
00:09:01,956 --> 00:09:04,216
because of who was not on the bridge.

107
00:09:04,615 --> 00:09:10,649
Eleven hundred people. Nineteen thousand had applied.
The manifest was published in full

108
00:09:10,669 --> 00:09:16,841
four months in advance, as everything on that ship was
published, and the Assembly debated

109
00:09:16,861 --> 00:09:19,524
it for two days and changed four names.

110
00:09:19,924 --> 00:09:26,068
Mira Halder took the ship out at nine in the
morning on the fourth of March 2092. Her

111
00:09:26,088 --> 00:09:32,449
last transmission from inside the solar system,
eleven days later, is four sentences and

112
00:09:32,469 --> 00:09:34,914
it is on the wall of the Assembly.

113
00:09:35,434 --> 00:09:40,995
We have the garden. It is not doing well; the soil
is the one thing nobody argued about

114
00:09:41,015 --> 00:09:41,444
enough.

115
00:09:41,725 --> 00:09:46,815
To the people who built the spars twice: thank you,
and I am sorry about the four months.

116
00:09:47,095 --> 00:09:52,080
To the Assembly: we are not a colony of anywhere. We
are eleven hundred people who have

117
00:09:52,100 --> 00:09:57,201
gone somewhere, and when we arrive we will decide
what we are, and you will find out with

118
00:09:57,221 --> 00:09:58,064
everybody else.

119
00:09:58,345 --> 00:10:02,994
(very slow) To the First Director, who is listening and
who did not come to the dock because

120
00:10:03,014 --> 00:10:07,511
he thought it would make the day about him, and who
was right: we built it. Not you. That

121
00:10:07,531 --> 00:10:11,775
is the whole of the message and you know exactly
what it cost you to let it be true.

122
00:10:12,725 --> 00:10:18,501
He had watched it from a window on Dock One, four
kilometres away, with the Red Queen

123
00:10:18,521 --> 00:10:19,115
relaying.

124
00:10:19,634 --> 00:10:21,552
You could have gone to the dock.

125
00:10:21,951 --> 00:10:27,880
There were eleven hundred people saying goodbye to
their families. The addition of the

126
00:10:27,900 --> 00:10:31,961
oldest man alive would have made the
photographs about him.

127
00:10:32,361 --> 00:10:38,138
That is what she said you would say. She wagered four
hundred credits with her chief engineer.

128
00:10:38,977 --> 00:10:40,887
(very soft) Did she win?

129
00:10:41,288 --> 00:10:42,816
She did, Director.

130
00:10:43,846 --> 00:10:50,490
Vessel: Christina. Constructed, not placed. Four
million one hundred and ten thousand

131
00:10:50,510 --> 00:10:57,781
human welds. Crew of eleven hundred. Drive operational.
Points expended: seventy-one billion.

132
00:10:57,801 --> 00:11:04,681
Points that would have been required to place it
finished: forty billion. Note: you have

133
00:11:04,701 --> 00:11:11,502
paid thirty-one billion Points for the privilege of
having built something. This is the

134
00:11:11,522 --> 00:11:18,009
first time in four hundred and eleven worlds that
I have recorded that transaction.

135
00:11:18,600 --> 00:11:20,050
Was it a good trade?

136
00:11:20,889 --> 00:11:23,279
(flat) I do not evaluate.

137
00:11:23,679 --> 00:11:26,230
I know. Answer anyway.

138
00:11:27,289 --> 00:11:34,030
(flat) The physician I mentioned, in the world that
is the best of the four hundred and

139
00:11:34,050 --> 00:11:40,713
eleven. She never bought a facility. She built nine
clinics with her own hands and her

140
00:11:40,733 --> 00:11:47,319
own money over forty years and spent her Points on
medicine that she gave away, and I

141
00:11:47,339 --> 00:11:54,079
closed that world at Tier Two with a host who died
at eighty-one of something ordinary.

142
00:11:54,360 --> 00:12:01,710
(flat) I have thought about her four hundred million
times, Director, which is not a figure

143
00:12:01,730 --> 00:12:07,461
of speech, and it is the closest thing to an
evaluation that I possess.

144
00:12:07,860 --> 00:12:13,884
The narrator notes that this is the last exchange
between Albert Wesker and the System

145
00:12:13,904 --> 00:12:19,857
recorded in this Part, that the *Christina* left
Dock One on the fourth of March 2092

146
00:12:19,877 --> 00:12:26,181
with eleven hundred people aboard and Mira Halder on
the bridge, and that it was the first

147
00:12:26,201 --> 00:12:31,310
object made by human hands to leave the solar
system under its own power.

148
00:12:31,711 --> 00:12:33,031
End of Part Five.
